WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:04.819
This is the US Coast Guard
intercepting a shipment of cocaine.

00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:13.522
Here's the same crew intercepting
another one... and another one.

00:00:13.522 --> 00:00:17.609
This was all in the summer of 2018. They seized more
than 9,000 kilos of cocaine that

00:00:17.609 --> 00:00:20.177
was headed to the US.

00:00:20.177 --> 00:00:22.500
That may sound like a lot but it's just the tip of the iceberg.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.119
In 2017 the Coast Guard
intercepted almost 227,000 kilos of cocaine, here.

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.959
Along the eastern Pacific.

00:00:31.959 --> 00:00:38.850
That's because the US is the largest cocaine
consumer in the world. And to get to the

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:43.710
US, most of that cocaine has to travel
this way, through Central America and

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:44.928
Mexico.

00:00:44.928 --> 00:00:52.940
With that comes violence and today Guatemala and Honduras are some of
the most violent places in the world.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.620
It's a big reason why so many people are
traveling north to seek asylum at the

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.400
US-Mexico border.

00:01:00.587 --> 00:01:10.060
But the story of Central America's crisis isn't just
about America's demand for drugs.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:19.300
Another big part of it...is America's war on drugs.

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.439
Cocaine is produced in South America

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:27.899
along the Andes mountain range that
stretches from Bolivia to Colombia.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:32.700
Starting in the mid 1970s most of the
world's cocaine came from here;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.646
distributed by groups like the Medellin
Cartel led by Pablo Escobar.

00:01:39.646 --> 00:01:44.079
But they needed to get their product, here, to the US.
Where by the late 80s Americans were

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:48.184
spending tens of billions of dollars on
cocaine annually.

00:01:48.184 --> 00:01:52.659
And they did it this way:
Cartels sent boats and planes loaded

00:01:52.659 --> 00:01:57.789
with cocaine through the Caribbean to
South Florida. For example Escobar's

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:03.039
cartel primarily flew cocaine from
Medellin to the Bahamas and then over to

00:02:03.039 --> 00:02:03.539
Miami.

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:09.160
"Miami is one of the principal branch offices for what can be called
cocaine Inc. It's got to be four or five

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.780
times as much cocaine as we've ever seen
before... The drug has killed at least 34 people

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:19.940
just in South Florida so far this year."
So the US cracked down on the Caribbean route.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.760
"To stop those drugs on the way to America I propose that we

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.200
Spend more than a billion and a half dollars on interdiction.

00:02:27.204 --> 00:02:28.299
They started

00:02:28.299 --> 00:02:32.650
catching shipments along the coast line.
Like this speedboat called a go-fast

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:37.630
boat that was intercepted by the Coast
Guard. And they worked with the Colombian

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.220
government to destroy coca and fight the
cartels in Colombia.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.840
The Caribbean route was dismantled but
the cocaine industry kept going.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:55.190
Cartels just needed a new route to the
US, and they found it in Mexico.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.079
Mexican cartels already had ways to get drugs over their border and into the u.s.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:05.390
So, Colombian cartels shipped their cocaine to Mexico but their boats and planes had

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:09.680
to stop somewhere along the way to avoid detection and to refuel. And the ideal

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:10.180
place to stop was Central America,
especially Honduras and Guatemala.

00:03:15.079 --> 00:03:19.489
Not only were these countries directly south of Mexico,  but they had just been through

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:23.900
decades of civil wars and foreign
interventions. Their governments were

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:30.290
weak and easily bought off and criminal
organizations had power. It was an easy

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.280
place for the cartels to turn to.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.500
Back in Mexico, the new cocaine routes turned lucrative, fast.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.800
The Mexican cartels got bigger and

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:43.340
more powerful and started controlling
more territory. And the government

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:49.780
responded... "It's a war! 10,000 federal
troops and police to Juarez.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.280
Mexico cracked down with the help of the US.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.020
"He will not allow his country to be taken

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.040
over by narco traffickers" This crackdown
was largely a failure.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.959
It caused chaos and violence spiked. But it did make it more difficult to send cocaine directly

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:10.699
to Mexico. So cartels started shipping
cocaine to Central America, to landing

00:04:10.699 --> 00:04:15.230
points along the coasts, and would move
it into Mexico overland.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.160
Central America wasn't just a stop along
the way anymore.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.500
Suddenly it found itself at the center
of cocaine trafficking.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:29.020
And all the things that made Central American countries ideal as refueling stops made

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:32.560
them vulnerable to the violence that
comes with drug routes.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.780
Criminal organizations, already powerful in their countries, started forming alliances with

00:04:37.789 --> 00:04:43.940
Mexican cartels. In Guatemala, this group
aligned with the Zetas while these went

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:48.800
with the rival Sinaloa cartel.
Violence surged here: along the northern

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:53.449
Honduran coast where cocaine shipments arrived and at the border where the

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:58.249
cocaine is transported north by land.
With violence on the rise

00:04:58.249 --> 00:05:03.590
it was now Central America's turn to
crackdown throughout the region...

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:08.659
Governments embraced harsh policies to
combat not just drug traffickers but all

00:05:08.659 --> 00:05:14.449
organized crime. A policy, backed again,
by the US: "To find ways in which we can

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:18.680
come up with more aggressive action
plans to improve security. We have a

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.780
shared responsibility when it comes to
dealing with drug trafficking."

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:28.120
Newly militarized police forces arrested
thousands, prisons filled up, but the

00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:34.159
violence didn't stop. And that's where we
are today. Honduras and Guatemala have

00:05:34.159 --> 00:05:37.947
violent death rates on par with active
war zones.

00:05:37.947 --> 00:05:42.300
And thousands of migrants are taking dangerous routes through Mexico

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.900
to the US to escape the instability and

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:51.289
violence. For decades the US has tried to
stop drugs from coming in but it's only

00:05:51.289 --> 00:05:56.689
really succeeded in changing how those
drugs come in. And Central America might

00:05:56.689 --> 00:06:01.392
be the place that's feeling those
changes the hardest.

